1
00:00:00,720 --> 00:00:03,840
All right, Chapter 4, Hemostasis and Related Disorders.

2
00:00:03,980 --> 00:00:05,780
This is our first section. It's an introduction.

3
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:08,500
It lays the framework for the rest of the chapter.

4
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,420
The idea here is that we need to recall that we've got blood vessels.

5
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:16,820
Obviously, the role of those blood vessels is to carry blood through the body.

6
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:20,280
Remember that the blood vessels line by endothelial cells,

7
00:00:20,460 --> 00:00:24,480
and the endothelial cells sit on top of basement membrane.

8
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:28,900
Again, endothelial cells sitting on top of basement membrane.

9
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:31,720
Now, the integrity of these blood vessels is essential.

10
00:00:31,940 --> 00:00:35,540
Without the integrity of these blood vessels, blood would not be able to reach the tissues.

11
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:38,020
We don't want blood leaking out here.

12
00:00:38,160 --> 00:00:43,100
We want blood going through the vessel and to reach the organs.

13
00:00:43,580 --> 00:00:47,780
If there happens to be damage to this tube, then the damage needs to be repaired,

14
00:00:48,260 --> 00:00:51,540
and the way by which we repair the damage is called hemostasis.

15
00:00:52,120 --> 00:00:56,840
Now, hemostasis works by forming a little area of a band-aid.

16
00:00:56,840 --> 00:00:59,460
For example, let's say that there's disruption here.

17
00:00:59,580 --> 00:01:03,840
This band-aid would form, and this band-aid is called a thrombus or a clot.

18
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:07,440
So wherever there's vessel injury, a clot or a thrombus will form.

19
00:01:07,900 --> 00:01:11,120
Now, in order to form that clot, we actually have two distinct stages.

20
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:16,860
The first stage is called primary hemostasis, and the second stage is called secondary hemostasis.

21
00:01:16,860 --> 00:01:21,800
In primary hemostasis, the goal is to form something called the platelet plug.

22
00:01:22,360 --> 00:01:28,800
And the platelet plug is just an adhesion and an aggregation of platelets in the region of the injury.

23
00:01:29,160 --> 00:01:32,640
Now, that platelet plug is weak, and so in order to stabilize that platelet plug,

24
00:01:32,720 --> 00:01:34,880
we have something called secondary hemostasis.

25
00:01:35,340 --> 00:01:39,300
Secondary hemostasis then comes in and stabilizes the platelet plug.

26
00:01:39,840 --> 00:01:42,560
And so this is a little bit of the normal.

27
00:01:42,560 --> 00:01:46,180
Now, let's just read together what we have written in the text.

28
00:01:46,640 --> 00:01:51,240
So I tell you that the integrity of the blood vessels is necessary to carry blood to the tissues,

29
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:55,720
that the damage to the wall of the vessel would be repaired by hemostasis,

30
00:01:56,060 --> 00:02:01,260
and that hemostasis involves a formation of a thrombus or a clot at the site of injury.

31
00:02:02,120 --> 00:02:05,320
Hemostasis, of course, occurs in two stages, primary and secondary.

32
00:02:05,880 --> 00:02:09,460
In primary hemostasis, we form a weak platelet plug,

33
00:02:09,460 --> 00:02:12,000
and the key thing here is that that platelet plug is weak.

34
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:17,100
Now, of course, the platelet plug is formed by an interaction between the platelets and the vessel wall.

35
00:02:17,640 --> 00:02:20,300
Then what comes in is secondary hemostasis comes in,

36
00:02:20,640 --> 00:02:25,200
and secondary hemostasis, the goal is to stabilize that platelet plug.

37
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:28,700
So again, secondary hemostasis stabilizes the platelet plug,

38
00:02:29,020 --> 00:02:32,800
and that's all mediated by that complex thing called the coagulation cascade.

39
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:37,260
So with this little bit of introduction, we can now move forward into the disorders.

40
00:02:37,260 --> 00:02:42,440
The first type of disorder that we want to talk about are those that are related to primary hemostasis.

41
00:02:43,040 --> 00:02:46,520
Now, of course, to understand these primary hemostasis disorders,

42
00:02:46,520 --> 00:02:51,500
you really have to understand normal primary hemostasis, and so let's go there next.

43
00:02:51,980 --> 00:02:55,060
Now, when there is disruption of the blood vessel,

44
00:02:55,060 --> 00:02:59,000
okay, so we've got a blood vessel, and again, lined by endothelial cells,

45
00:02:59,080 --> 00:03:04,440
and of course, the blood vessel endothelial cells sit on top of a basement membrane.

46
00:03:04,900 --> 00:03:07,200
When there is disruption, as I've drawn here,

47
00:03:07,520 --> 00:03:10,620
the very first thing is a little bit of a knee-jerk reaction.

48
00:03:11,080 --> 00:03:18,160
So step one of this primary hemostasis will be that there will be transient vasoconstriction of the damaged vessels.

49
00:03:18,600 --> 00:03:22,220
Now, this is very important to note because actually examiners like to go after it.

50
00:03:22,220 --> 00:03:26,160
Step one, the blood vessel will actually rapidly constrict.

51
00:03:26,520 --> 00:03:28,160
I think of it as a knee-jerk reaction.

52
00:03:28,380 --> 00:03:31,420
When you get a little bit of an injury, you kind of pull away from something.

53
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:33,620
I don't know, if you touch something hot, you'll pull your hand away.

54
00:03:33,940 --> 00:03:37,160
When the blood vessel gets damaged, it just clamps down very quickly.

55
00:03:38,020 --> 00:03:42,760
And that perhaps is to decrease the amount of blood supply going to the region for a moment

56
00:03:42,760 --> 00:03:45,820
before hemostasis can kick in,

57
00:03:46,140 --> 00:03:50,260
or it might also be that the blood vessel is sort of just shaking itself to awaken.

58
00:03:50,260 --> 00:03:57,840
Now, you need to know for the purposes of examinations that this step one is actually mediated by two things.

59
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:04,280
The first is neural stimulation, so it's a neural reflex for the blood vessel to clamp down when there's initial injury.

60
00:04:04,800 --> 00:04:06,980
And number two, and perhaps a little more high yield,

61
00:04:07,280 --> 00:04:11,620
is that the endothelial cells themselves have a molecule called endothelion.

62
00:04:11,620 --> 00:04:15,600
So that when this endothelial cell, for example, gets disrupted,

63
00:04:16,060 --> 00:04:17,340
endothelion will be released,

64
00:04:17,680 --> 00:04:22,260
and that will cause a very quick transient vasoconstriction of the damaged vessel.

65
00:04:23,140 --> 00:04:26,720
Now, the next step is that von Willebrand's factor will come in

66
00:04:26,720 --> 00:04:29,980
and will line the surface of the area of the disruption

67
00:04:29,980 --> 00:04:34,540
at the level of the subendothelial collagen.

68
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:37,900
Now, you realize that when the endothelial cells are disrupted,

69
00:04:37,900 --> 00:04:41,760
we've now exposed both the basement membrane, which contains collagen,

70
00:04:42,060 --> 00:04:45,180
as well as the connective tissue underneath, which contains collagen.

71
00:04:45,680 --> 00:04:49,700
That collagen then allows for von Willebrand's factor to actually bind.

72
00:04:50,060 --> 00:04:51,780
So von Willebrand's factor will bind,

73
00:04:52,180 --> 00:04:55,300
and then von Willebrand's factor will act as a linker molecule

74
00:04:55,300 --> 00:04:57,860
for the platelets to bind to this region.

75
00:04:58,220 --> 00:05:03,080
And the way by which the platelets bind is by using a receptor called GP1B.

76
00:05:03,080 --> 00:05:07,400
So the platelets will use GP1B to bind von Willebrand's factor,

77
00:05:07,600 --> 00:05:11,280
and that is called platelet adhesion, and it is step number two.

78
00:05:11,680 --> 00:05:16,780
Platelet adhesion, the platelets bind von Willebrand's factor via GP1B.

79
00:05:17,200 --> 00:05:19,540
Important to note, for the purposes of examinations,

80
00:05:19,920 --> 00:05:22,080
where does von Willebrand's factor come from?

81
00:05:22,480 --> 00:05:24,380
Von Willebrand's factor comes from two sources.

82
00:05:24,680 --> 00:05:28,180
Number one, the platelet itself has some von Willebrand's factor within it,

83
00:05:28,180 --> 00:05:30,420
but number two, and the most important,

84
00:05:30,780 --> 00:05:35,240
is that the endothelial cells have von Willebrand's factor themselves.

85
00:05:35,640 --> 00:05:38,080
And so the vast majority of the von Willebrand's factor

86
00:05:38,080 --> 00:05:40,200
actually comes from the endothelial cell.

87
00:05:40,600 --> 00:05:42,280
Unfortunately, that's not sufficient.

88
00:05:42,440 --> 00:05:43,960
You do need to know one more thing,

89
00:05:44,260 --> 00:05:46,500
and that is that within the endothelial cell,

90
00:05:46,800 --> 00:05:52,000
it's the Weibull-Pilotti body that actually holds the von Willebrand's factor.

91
00:05:52,360 --> 00:05:55,760
Remember that the Weibull-Pilotti body is something that we learned about

92
00:05:55,760 --> 00:05:58,780
in normal histology.

93
00:05:59,420 --> 00:06:03,340
It is a body that is viewable by electron microscopy,

94
00:06:03,560 --> 00:06:08,160
and it contains many different substances necessary for endothelial function.

95
00:06:08,600 --> 00:06:12,460
The way I remember the two most important things within the Weibull-Pilotti bodies

96
00:06:12,460 --> 00:06:15,480
is to use the letters to remind me of what's actually in them.

97
00:06:15,840 --> 00:06:18,840
So, for example, the P reminds me that

98
00:06:18,840 --> 00:06:23,620
one of the important molecules in the Weibull-Pilotti body is P-selectin.

99
00:06:23,620 --> 00:06:28,720
And remember, P-selectin, its role was important

100
00:06:28,720 --> 00:06:33,500
when we talked about the selectin speed bumps in our inflammation discussion.

101
00:06:33,980 --> 00:06:36,200
So, I'm not going to go into the details here,

102
00:06:36,500 --> 00:06:39,100
but those P-selectins were important there.

103
00:06:39,620 --> 00:06:43,740
And the W helps me to remember that von Willebrand's factor

104
00:06:43,740 --> 00:06:46,980
is also contained within the Weibull-Pilotti bodies.

105
00:06:47,560 --> 00:06:48,540
Now, that was step two.

106
00:06:48,920 --> 00:06:50,300
Once this binding occurs,

107
00:06:50,300 --> 00:06:53,260
then the next thing that happens is that the platelets get activated,

108
00:06:53,460 --> 00:06:54,540
they undergo shape change,

109
00:06:54,660 --> 00:06:55,860
and then they degranulate.

110
00:06:56,300 --> 00:06:57,520
And so that is step three.

111
00:06:57,900 --> 00:07:00,620
So, adhesion of the platelets to von Willebrand's factor

112
00:07:00,620 --> 00:07:03,160
will then cause a shape change within the platelets,

113
00:07:03,440 --> 00:07:06,500
which then allows for the dumping of mediators

114
00:07:06,500 --> 00:07:09,860
that are going to be necessary for moving this process forward.

115
00:07:10,200 --> 00:07:11,820
And there are two important mediators

116
00:07:11,820 --> 00:07:13,640
that are going to arise from the platelets.

117
00:07:14,020 --> 00:07:16,300
One is going to be ADP,

118
00:07:16,300 --> 00:07:19,770
and ADP is essential,

119
00:07:20,250 --> 00:07:23,570
and the second is going to be thromboxane A2.

120
00:07:24,570 --> 00:07:26,950
And remember that thromboxane A2

121
00:07:26,950 --> 00:07:30,990
is a derivative of platelet cyclooxygenase,

122
00:07:31,110 --> 00:07:31,990
and that's high yield.

123
00:07:32,430 --> 00:07:34,850
ADP is present within the dense corp ranules,

124
00:07:35,710 --> 00:07:40,130
and thromboxane A2 is a derivative of platelet cyclooxygenase.

125
00:07:40,510 --> 00:07:43,610
Now, the important things about ADP and thromboxane A2,

126
00:07:43,610 --> 00:07:47,810
ADP induces the platelets to express another receptor,

127
00:07:48,210 --> 00:07:55,590
and the other receptor is going to be something called GP2B3A.

128
00:07:56,370 --> 00:08:01,610
Now, a GP2B3A receptor is essential for platelet aggregation.

129
00:08:02,110 --> 00:08:05,310
Aggregation is when all the platelets join together

130
00:08:05,310 --> 00:08:07,510
at this particular spot.

131
00:08:07,930 --> 00:08:10,170
Remember that adhesion was the platelet binding

132
00:08:10,170 --> 00:08:15,010
to the area of exposed subendothelial collagen via von Willebrand's factor.

133
00:08:15,370 --> 00:08:18,130
Aggregation is when the platelets begin to aggregate

134
00:08:18,130 --> 00:08:21,350
on top of those adhesed platelets.

135
00:08:21,910 --> 00:08:25,610
So ADP is essential for the expression of GP2B3A.

136
00:08:26,130 --> 00:08:29,050
And then TXA2, thromboxane A2,

137
00:08:29,310 --> 00:08:33,850
will be the signal that will allow for further platelet aggregation.

138
00:08:34,190 --> 00:08:35,850
So platelets will begin to aggregate here,

139
00:08:35,950 --> 00:08:37,130
and one important thing to note

140
00:08:37,130 --> 00:08:40,550
is that the important linker molecule that the platelets use

141
00:08:40,550 --> 00:08:43,390
to aggregate to one another is called fibrinogen.

142
00:08:44,070 --> 00:08:47,170
So this important linker molecule here is called fibrinogen,

143
00:08:47,590 --> 00:08:49,750
and that, again, is essential to know as well.

144
00:08:50,310 --> 00:08:53,290
Okay, so with that in mind, let's read a little bit of our text.

145
00:08:53,690 --> 00:08:57,110
So I say that primary hemostasis occurs via four steps.

146
00:08:57,430 --> 00:08:58,390
In the very first step,

147
00:08:58,450 --> 00:09:01,050
we get transient vasoconstriction of the damaged vessel.

148
00:09:01,050 --> 00:09:05,930
This is mediated by reflex neural stimulation and endothelion,

149
00:09:06,070 --> 00:09:07,670
which comes from the endothelial cells.

150
00:09:08,210 --> 00:09:10,410
Step two, you get the platelet adhesion

151
00:09:10,410 --> 00:09:12,410
to the surface of the disrupted vessels.

152
00:09:12,550 --> 00:09:14,470
And again, von Willebrand's factor will bind

153
00:09:14,470 --> 00:09:15,950
that subendothelial collagen.

154
00:09:16,370 --> 00:09:18,810
The platelets will bind that using GP1B.

155
00:09:19,310 --> 00:09:21,170
Where does von Willebrand's factor come from?

156
00:09:21,250 --> 00:09:24,590
From the Weibol-Pilotti bodies and from the platelets,

157
00:09:24,690 --> 00:09:27,010
in particular the alpha granules of the platelets.

158
00:09:27,550 --> 00:09:29,050
Now then, that will cause platelet degranulation.

159
00:09:29,050 --> 00:09:32,210
So the adhesion will cause a shape change,

160
00:09:32,350 --> 00:09:33,950
which then results in degranulation

161
00:09:33,950 --> 00:09:35,710
with release of multiple mediators.

162
00:09:36,110 --> 00:09:38,770
Remember that ADP, which comes from the dense granules,

163
00:09:38,930 --> 00:09:41,950
then promotes the exposure of GP2B3A

164
00:09:41,950 --> 00:09:43,530
on the receptor of the platelets,

165
00:09:43,890 --> 00:09:47,470
and thromboxyne A2, a derivative of cyclooxygenase,

166
00:09:47,550 --> 00:09:48,510
very high yield again,

167
00:09:48,670 --> 00:09:50,930
derivative of platelet cyclooxygenase,

168
00:09:51,390 --> 00:09:53,310
further promotes platelet aggregation.

169
00:09:53,790 --> 00:09:57,050
And platelet aggregation is going to occur via GP2B3A

170
00:09:57,050 --> 00:10:00,270
using fibrinogen as a linker molecule.

171
00:10:00,770 --> 00:10:02,910
Now important to note, and I've already stated,

172
00:10:03,110 --> 00:10:05,610
that this results in a formation of a platelet plug.

173
00:10:06,010 --> 00:10:07,710
That platelet plug is weak.

174
00:10:08,150 --> 00:10:10,030
It doesn't have the ability to withstand

175
00:10:10,030 --> 00:10:11,270
a tremendous amount of force.

176
00:10:12,070 --> 00:10:15,050
And so in order to further stabilize that platelet plug,

177
00:10:15,210 --> 00:10:17,050
we're going to undergo secondary hemostasis,

178
00:10:17,830 --> 00:10:21,330
which basically is the job of the coagulation cascade.

179
00:10:21,730 --> 00:10:23,290
But of course in this particular section,

180
00:10:23,290 --> 00:10:25,430
we're dealing with disorders of primary hemostasis,

181
00:10:25,810 --> 00:10:29,230
so we're not going to focus on the coagulation cascade yet.

182
00:10:29,350 --> 00:10:30,710
That's going to come up a little bit later.

183
00:10:31,390 --> 00:10:33,430
Now what about disorders of primary hemostasis?

184
00:10:33,550 --> 00:10:34,790
How can we think about those?

185
00:10:35,110 --> 00:10:37,490
Well those are basically, for the most part,

186
00:10:37,550 --> 00:10:38,890
not always, but for the most part,

187
00:10:38,970 --> 00:10:40,910
they're going to be due to abnormalities in platelets.

188
00:10:41,050 --> 00:10:42,470
And there's two types of abnormalities

189
00:10:42,470 --> 00:10:43,570
that we can have in platelets.

190
00:10:44,030 --> 00:10:45,970
We can have a quantitative abnormality

191
00:10:45,970 --> 00:10:47,670
where we don't have enough platelets.

192
00:10:47,790 --> 00:10:48,930
The quantity is bad.

193
00:10:49,250 --> 00:10:51,110
Or we can have a qualitative problem

194
00:10:51,110 --> 00:10:53,650
where the quality of the platelets is no good,

195
00:10:53,750 --> 00:10:55,590
but we have enough platelets, for example.

196
00:10:56,310 --> 00:10:58,750
And so these are the two ways we want to think about

197
00:10:58,750 --> 00:11:00,330
disorders of primary hemostasis

198
00:11:00,330 --> 00:11:04,910
when we're in the context of abnormalities of the platelets.

199
00:11:05,470 --> 00:11:08,290
Quantitative disorders and qualitative disorders.

200
00:11:08,830 --> 00:11:11,410
Now when patients have disorders of primary hemostasis,

201
00:11:11,530 --> 00:11:15,710
they're going to present with some very classical signs and symptoms.

202
00:11:15,830 --> 00:11:17,890
And the most important is that

203
00:11:17,890 --> 00:11:20,310
they're going to get mucosal and skin bleeding.

204
00:11:20,310 --> 00:11:24,210
This is the predominant picture of a patient

205
00:11:24,210 --> 00:11:26,750
with a disorder of primary hemostasis.

206
00:11:27,230 --> 00:11:28,930
So what are some examples of mucosal bleeding?

207
00:11:29,070 --> 00:11:30,630
Well, any mucosal site can bleed.

208
00:11:30,730 --> 00:11:33,530
For example, the patient can have nose bleeding or epistaxis.

209
00:11:34,030 --> 00:11:36,330
They can have coughing up a blood or hemopysis,

210
00:11:36,530 --> 00:11:38,890
GI bleeding, hematuria with blood in the urine,

211
00:11:39,310 --> 00:11:41,490
or menorrhagia, heavy menstrual bleeding

212
00:11:41,490 --> 00:11:44,730
because the endometrium is also a mucosal surface.

213
00:11:45,210 --> 00:11:46,830
So these are some classic signs and symptoms,

214
00:11:46,970 --> 00:11:49,310
and actually the most common is epistaxis.

215
00:11:49,310 --> 00:11:51,570
And that's why I sort of went after that one

216
00:11:51,570 --> 00:11:53,510
and highlighted it for you as I was talking.

217
00:11:54,090 --> 00:11:58,790
Now one of the feared complications of having a low platelet count

218
00:11:58,790 --> 00:12:00,610
is intracranial bleeding.

219
00:12:01,010 --> 00:12:03,310
And this occurs when there is severe thrombocytopenia.

220
00:12:04,410 --> 00:12:06,870
So that's something to just bury away in the back of our minds.

221
00:12:07,130 --> 00:12:09,310
That, you know, mucosal bleeding is not so bad.

222
00:12:09,350 --> 00:12:10,150
We can deal with it.

223
00:12:10,310 --> 00:12:11,690
But the feared complication is that

224
00:12:11,690 --> 00:12:14,190
as the platelet count goes down, for example,

225
00:12:14,190 --> 00:12:16,630
if there was a quantitative problem with platelets,

226
00:12:17,130 --> 00:12:19,430
then intracranial bleeding would,

227
00:12:19,790 --> 00:12:21,790
the risk of intracranial bleeding would increase

228
00:12:21,790 --> 00:12:25,230
and we'd become more and more worried about that particular complication.

229
00:12:25,970 --> 00:12:29,510
Now we can also get skin bleeding with disorders of primary hemostasis.

230
00:12:29,950 --> 00:12:34,330
For example, patients can develop petechiae or pinpoint bleeding in the skin.

231
00:12:34,710 --> 00:12:37,170
They can develop purpura, which are slightly larger,

232
00:12:37,330 --> 00:12:39,950
measuring approximately 3 millimeters or greater.

233
00:12:39,950 --> 00:12:42,170
And finally, they can develop ecchymosis,

234
00:12:42,470 --> 00:12:45,010
which measure approximately greater than 1 centimeter.

235
00:12:45,430 --> 00:12:47,990
And so these are varying sizes of bleeding within the skin.

236
00:12:48,350 --> 00:12:51,650
Now I'd like to alert you to the point that there is an error in your text here

237
00:12:51,650 --> 00:12:54,910
and that the size of purpura and ecchymosis has been reversed.

238
00:12:55,370 --> 00:12:57,590
So again, purpura are greater than 3 millimeters

239
00:12:57,590 --> 00:13:01,690
and ecchymosis are areas of bleed greater than 1 centimeter in the skin.

240
00:13:02,250 --> 00:13:07,330
Now another symptom of skin bleeding might be easy bruising.

241
00:13:07,330 --> 00:13:09,550
So that would have been patients with thrombocytopenia

242
00:13:09,550 --> 00:13:13,430
or patients, why don't I say, with disorders of primary hemostasis

243
00:13:13,430 --> 00:13:15,170
would also exhibit easy bruising.

244
00:13:15,890 --> 00:13:17,850
Now one of the important things to note,

245
00:13:17,950 --> 00:13:20,770
that when we talk about petechiae, ecchymosis, and purpura,

246
00:13:21,350 --> 00:13:22,610
when we get this type of bleeding,

247
00:13:22,730 --> 00:13:26,870
we're really talking, it really is a sign of thrombocytopenia.

248
00:13:27,210 --> 00:13:28,350
Now what I mean by that is,

249
00:13:28,430 --> 00:13:31,590
remember when we talked about the disorders that could arise,

250
00:13:31,590 --> 00:13:35,290
we said there can be a quantitative disorder of platelets

251
00:13:35,290 --> 00:13:37,870
or a qualitative disorder of platelets.

252
00:13:38,290 --> 00:13:40,830
Well patients who have qualitative disorders of platelets,

253
00:13:41,010 --> 00:13:42,790
they normally don't get petechiae.

254
00:13:43,250 --> 00:13:45,690
However, patients who have quantitative,

255
00:13:46,010 --> 00:13:48,590
where the number is down, they get petechiae.

256
00:13:48,930 --> 00:13:49,830
And so that's a little,

257
00:13:50,090 --> 00:13:52,650
that's just a little side note to be aware of.

258
00:13:53,630 --> 00:13:55,190
Here's what the petechiae look like.

259
00:13:55,370 --> 00:13:56,770
They're these little tiny bleeds

260
00:13:56,770 --> 00:13:59,230
that are exhibited on the skin here.

261
00:13:59,730 --> 00:14:01,830
And so this is the classic image of petechiae.

262
00:14:02,030 --> 00:14:03,830
You can see here that perhaps we're getting

263
00:14:03,830 --> 00:14:07,850
into the possibility of having a little bit of ecchymosis here

264
00:14:07,850 --> 00:14:10,550
where the bleeds may be slightly larger as well.

265
00:14:11,210 --> 00:14:13,670
When a patient, sorry, not a platelet,

266
00:14:13,830 --> 00:14:18,470
when a patient has a disorder of primary hemostasis,

267
00:14:18,610 --> 00:14:21,170
you've got mucosal bleeding, you've got skin bleeding,

268
00:14:21,570 --> 00:14:23,750
then you're going to want to look at some laboratory studies

269
00:14:23,750 --> 00:14:26,230
in order to try to determine where the problem is.

270
00:14:26,230 --> 00:14:28,870
So, for example, the platelet count can be very helpful.

271
00:14:29,050 --> 00:14:30,510
It tells us how many platelets there are.

272
00:14:30,830 --> 00:14:34,090
If we had a qualitative, i.e., problem with the number of platelets,

273
00:14:34,390 --> 00:14:36,330
of course, the platelet count would reveal that.

274
00:14:36,710 --> 00:14:41,790
The normal platelet count is anywhere from 150,000 to 400,000,

275
00:14:42,190 --> 00:14:45,350
150,000 to 400,000 per microliter.

276
00:14:45,950 --> 00:14:49,910
Less than that, we would begin to potentially see problems,

277
00:14:50,010 --> 00:14:53,630
especially when the platelet count is less than 50K or 50,000.

278
00:14:53,630 --> 00:14:57,490
The bleeding time is also a useful laboratory study.

279
00:14:58,170 --> 00:15:02,090
The bleeding time, basically, the idea here is that you prick the patient

280
00:15:02,090 --> 00:15:04,710
and you see how long it takes them before they stop bleeding.

281
00:15:04,990 --> 00:15:07,030
The normal is anywhere from 2 to 7 minutes.

282
00:15:07,230 --> 00:15:11,390
So, normally, in about 2 up to 7 minutes, the patient should stop bleeding.

283
00:15:11,790 --> 00:15:15,550
However, when there's quantitative problems or qualitative problems,

284
00:15:15,550 --> 00:15:18,010
the bleeding will be prolonged.

285
00:15:18,610 --> 00:15:19,750
And so, that's the bleeding time.

286
00:15:19,990 --> 00:15:24,250
Now, the reason why I argue whether that's useful is perhaps most places in the country

287
00:15:24,250 --> 00:15:25,550
wouldn't be doing this anymore.

288
00:15:25,950 --> 00:15:28,930
It's a little bit of a primitive test, but it does show up on examinations,

289
00:15:29,030 --> 00:15:31,310
and so I kept it in your text.

290
00:15:32,090 --> 00:15:35,310
The next way by which we could assess the patient's platelets is to look at them,

291
00:15:35,710 --> 00:15:37,050
and we can do that by blood smear.

292
00:15:37,270 --> 00:15:39,210
You take the patient's blood, you smear it on a slide,

293
00:15:39,270 --> 00:15:41,050
and you get a peek at what the platelets look like.

294
00:15:41,050 --> 00:15:44,370
It helps you to tell how many platelets are present.

295
00:15:44,590 --> 00:15:48,250
We can assess the number of platelets, at least in rough estimate.

296
00:15:48,810 --> 00:15:51,710
We can assess the number of platelets by looking at them on the slide,

297
00:15:51,870 --> 00:15:54,030
and we can also assess the size of the platelets,

298
00:15:54,090 --> 00:15:57,350
and you'll see that there will be some disorders where the size of the platelets will matter,

299
00:15:57,850 --> 00:16:00,310
and so that is how the blood smear would be helpful.

300
00:16:00,810 --> 00:16:04,230
And then, finally, we can do a bone marrow biopsy to assess the platelets,

301
00:16:04,330 --> 00:16:07,730
and the way this works is that remember that the megakaryocytes are the cells

302
00:16:07,730 --> 00:16:09,150
that are actually producing the platelets.

303
00:16:09,150 --> 00:16:12,730
So we can ask the question, if, for example, platelet count is low,

304
00:16:13,030 --> 00:16:14,170
are there megakaryocytes?

305
00:16:14,290 --> 00:16:17,250
Maybe it's that we don't have megakaryocytes in the bone marrow,

306
00:16:17,330 --> 00:16:19,310
and that's why the platelet count is low.

307
00:16:19,730 --> 00:16:21,950
So the bone marrow biopsy is also very helpful.

308
00:16:23,550 --> 00:16:26,790
The first of the disorders that we want to discuss is called ITP.

309
00:16:27,450 --> 00:16:31,470
This is an autoimmune production of IgG against platelet antigens.

310
00:16:31,570 --> 00:16:34,750
For example, one of the antigens on platelets is GP2B3A.

311
00:16:34,750 --> 00:16:42,750
Remember, GP2B3A is the receptor that's used for cross-linking of the platelets via fibrinogen.

312
00:16:43,430 --> 00:16:46,350
Remember that story we talked about a little bit earlier.

313
00:16:46,970 --> 00:16:52,130
Now, the point here, however, is that the patients get IgG against platelet antigens.

314
00:16:52,710 --> 00:16:54,750
This is the most common cause of thrombocytopenia,

315
00:16:55,530 --> 00:16:57,530
thrombocytopenia low platelets in the blood.

316
00:16:57,850 --> 00:17:01,770
This is the most common cause of thrombocytopenia in both children and adults,

317
00:17:01,770 --> 00:17:03,530
so it's very important to be aware of it.

318
00:17:04,030 --> 00:17:09,210
Now, the antibodies in this particular case, those IgG antibodies that are causing the disease,

319
00:17:09,350 --> 00:17:11,750
are actually produced by the plasma cells in the spleen.

320
00:17:12,410 --> 00:17:14,990
And interestingly, what happens is these antibodies are produced,

321
00:17:15,110 --> 00:17:17,110
they go out into the blood, they bind the platelets,

322
00:17:17,190 --> 00:17:21,590
and when they bind the platelets, the macrophages within the spleen

323
00:17:21,590 --> 00:17:24,510
actually eat those antibody-bound platelets.

324
00:17:24,510 --> 00:17:27,070
So, the antibodies are made in the spleen,

325
00:17:27,550 --> 00:17:34,530
and the platelets that are bound by the antibodies are actually consumed by the macrophages in the spleen.

326
00:17:34,650 --> 00:17:40,210
So, the spleen is making the antibody as well as consuming the platelets that are bound to the antibody.

327
00:17:40,610 --> 00:17:45,950
And, of course, when the platelets are consumed, that's what results in the thrombocytopenia.

328
00:17:46,550 --> 00:17:50,470
Now, the acute form is that form that arises in children.

329
00:17:50,630 --> 00:17:52,970
So, otherwise stated, why don't we take a step back?

330
00:17:52,970 --> 00:17:56,650
This disorder can be divided into something called the acute form and the chronic form.

331
00:17:57,310 --> 00:17:59,750
In the acute form, which classically arises in children,

332
00:17:59,950 --> 00:18:07,290
you see thrombocytopenia weeks after a viral infection or immunization.

333
00:18:07,670 --> 00:18:10,950
Of course, it's due to autoantibody production of an IgG,

334
00:18:11,110 --> 00:18:14,770
which is binding the platelets and resulting in consumption of the platelets.

335
00:18:15,090 --> 00:18:16,790
And this is a self-limited disorder.

336
00:18:17,090 --> 00:18:19,750
It usually resolves by itself within weeks of presentation.

337
00:18:19,750 --> 00:18:27,210
The only thing we have to do is support the patient if the platelet count goes down to a very low value, for example.

338
00:18:28,070 --> 00:18:30,470
In the chronic form, which normally arises in adults,

339
00:18:30,890 --> 00:18:33,510
this is classically seen in women of childbearing age.

340
00:18:33,590 --> 00:18:35,290
Again, it's an autoimmune type of disorder.

341
00:18:35,690 --> 00:18:37,690
And it can be primary or secondary.

342
00:18:38,290 --> 00:18:40,250
Now, primary, we don't really know the cause.

343
00:18:40,330 --> 00:18:43,690
In secondary, it's associated with another disorder, for example, lupus.

344
00:18:43,690 --> 00:18:47,250
Remember in our discussions of lupus that I told you that in lupus,

345
00:18:47,510 --> 00:18:50,970
one of the interesting things about these patients is that most patients with lupus

346
00:18:50,970 --> 00:18:55,450
will have an antibody against something, against one of the cells in the blood.

347
00:18:55,770 --> 00:18:59,250
For example, patients with lupus can produce antibodies against their red blood cells.

348
00:18:59,390 --> 00:19:01,550
They can produce antibodies against their white cells.

349
00:19:01,950 --> 00:19:04,530
They can also produce antibodies against their platelets.

350
00:19:04,530 --> 00:19:10,530
And so lupus is a very important association with us, with ITP,

351
00:19:11,350 --> 00:19:13,790
especially as a secondary cause of ITP.

352
00:19:14,310 --> 00:19:16,750
Now, again, remember that patients who have the chronic form

353
00:19:16,750 --> 00:19:19,510
are going to have this disease for a long period of time,

354
00:19:19,570 --> 00:19:21,150
and that's why it's called the chronic form.

355
00:19:21,570 --> 00:19:24,530
And so it would not be uncommon for these patients to actually have the disease

356
00:19:24,530 --> 00:19:27,390
and eventually to become pregnant just as part of their normal life.

357
00:19:27,790 --> 00:19:30,290
When they become pregnant, it's important to note that

358
00:19:30,290 --> 00:19:35,930
because this is an IgG-mediated disease, the IgG can actually cross the placenta.

359
00:19:36,070 --> 00:19:38,270
IgG has the ability to cross the placenta,

360
00:19:38,670 --> 00:19:42,210
and that can result in thrombocytopenia in the offspring.

361
00:19:42,650 --> 00:19:43,870
Of course, that would be short-lived

362
00:19:43,870 --> 00:19:46,770
because eventually the antibodies would be consumed over time,

363
00:19:47,070 --> 00:19:50,350
and then there would be no more IgG antibody once the baby was born.

364
00:19:50,690 --> 00:19:54,750
But it is important to note that women who have the chronic form

365
00:19:54,750 --> 00:19:57,270
or the long-standing form of ITP

366
00:19:57,270 --> 00:20:01,250
can actually have short-lived thrombocytopenia in their offspring.

367
00:20:01,830 --> 00:20:03,010
Now, what are we going to see on labs?

368
00:20:03,090 --> 00:20:07,650
Well, obviously, we've got idiopathic or immune thrombocytopenia.

369
00:20:08,350 --> 00:20:11,490
Thrombocyto refers to the platelets, and penia means that they're low.

370
00:20:11,790 --> 00:20:13,250
So the platelet count's going to be low

371
00:20:13,250 --> 00:20:14,930
because the platelets are being consumed.

372
00:20:15,330 --> 00:20:18,190
Often, it's less than 50,000 per microliter.

373
00:20:18,710 --> 00:20:22,190
At the same time, I'd like you to know that the PT and PTT are going to be normal.

374
00:20:22,330 --> 00:20:24,850
We haven't touched the coagulation cascade here.

375
00:20:24,850 --> 00:20:28,090
All of the coagulation factors are going to be normal,

376
00:20:28,530 --> 00:20:31,870
and therefore, the PT and the PTT are also going to be normal.

377
00:20:32,230 --> 00:20:35,530
Finally, because we're destroying platelets outside of the bone marrow,

378
00:20:35,810 --> 00:20:39,250
the bone marrow is actually going to respond by trying to produce more platelets.

379
00:20:39,690 --> 00:20:41,610
And as it tries to produce more platelets,

380
00:20:41,710 --> 00:20:44,770
we're going to see a hyperplasia of the megakureocytes.

381
00:20:44,870 --> 00:20:48,930
We're going to see an increased number of megakureocytes on the bone marrow biopsy.

382
00:20:49,330 --> 00:20:51,970
The initial treatment of this disorder is corticosteroids.

383
00:20:51,970 --> 00:20:55,770
If a child needs corticosteroids, they tend to respond very well.

384
00:20:56,090 --> 00:20:58,390
Important to note that the adults with the chronic form,

385
00:20:58,470 --> 00:21:01,590
they can respond initially, but they often relapse

386
00:21:01,590 --> 00:21:05,010
despite the fact that they've been treated with corticosteroids.

387
00:21:05,830 --> 00:21:08,130
One of the interesting things that we can do

388
00:21:08,130 --> 00:21:12,010
if a patient has a symptomatically low platelet count,

389
00:21:12,250 --> 00:21:14,590
let's say the patient has a very low platelet count,

390
00:21:14,750 --> 00:21:16,910
and they're starting to bleed from multiple sites,

391
00:21:17,190 --> 00:21:19,790
and now you're worried that the feared complication

392
00:21:19,790 --> 00:21:21,650
of intracranial bleeding might occur,

393
00:21:21,990 --> 00:21:23,450
and you want to raise the platelet count.

394
00:21:23,510 --> 00:21:27,010
Well, one way to raise the platelet count would be to give the patient IVIG.

395
00:21:27,650 --> 00:21:28,650
Now, what is IVIG?

396
00:21:29,090 --> 00:21:32,270
IV implies that you give it intravenously,

397
00:21:32,770 --> 00:21:35,190
and IG is the immunoglobulin.

398
00:21:35,450 --> 00:21:38,490
So what you do is you give the patient intravenous immunoglobulin.

399
00:21:38,590 --> 00:21:39,570
Now, why would that help?

400
00:21:39,910 --> 00:21:41,830
Well, if you give the patient a ton of immunoglobulin,

401
00:21:41,910 --> 00:21:44,770
what's going to happen is you're going to have the splenic macrophages

402
00:21:44,770 --> 00:21:48,750
to start eating that immunoglobulin instead of the immunoglobulin

403
00:21:48,750 --> 00:21:50,010
that's bound to the platelets.

404
00:21:50,250 --> 00:21:53,470
So it's like throwing a stick at a dog so that it goes the other way.

405
00:21:53,710 --> 00:21:56,330
You throw these little immunoglobulin sticks at the spleen,

406
00:21:56,630 --> 00:21:58,490
and the spleen will start eating those sticks

407
00:21:58,490 --> 00:22:02,590
instead of eating the platelet-bound immunoglobulin.

408
00:22:02,670 --> 00:22:06,230
Basically, you're going to overrun the spleen with all of this IVIG

409
00:22:06,230 --> 00:22:08,950
so that it sits there and consumes the IVIG

410
00:22:08,950 --> 00:22:11,730
and leaves the platelets alone for a little while.

411
00:22:11,730 --> 00:22:14,930
And, of course, you can imagine that once the IVIG gets consumed,

412
00:22:15,350 --> 00:22:19,270
then the spleen will again start eating the immunoglobulin-bound platelets.

413
00:22:19,730 --> 00:22:23,010
So the effect is short-lived, and that's important to know as well.

414
00:22:23,090 --> 00:22:25,490
This is just used to raise the platelet count

415
00:22:25,490 --> 00:22:26,830
when there's symptomatic bleeding.

416
00:22:27,130 --> 00:22:29,390
Basically, you're trying to defend the patient's platelets

417
00:22:29,390 --> 00:22:32,450
from being eaten for a short period of time.

418
00:22:33,050 --> 00:22:36,070
The final treatment and sort of the very last stage treatment

419
00:22:36,070 --> 00:22:37,610
would be to do splenectomy.

420
00:22:37,990 --> 00:22:41,370
Very high yield to know that when a patient gets splenectomy,

421
00:22:41,370 --> 00:22:44,990
you're actually going to eliminate both the source of the antibody

422
00:22:44,990 --> 00:22:47,390
because, remember, the plasma cells in the spleen

423
00:22:47,390 --> 00:22:49,750
were actually making the antibody,

424
00:22:50,070 --> 00:22:52,270
and you're going to eliminate the site of destruction

425
00:22:52,270 --> 00:22:54,850
because, remember, that the macrophages of the spleen

426
00:22:54,850 --> 00:22:59,450
were the cells that were actually eating the antibody-coated platelets.

427
00:22:59,930 --> 00:23:02,650
So splenectomy kind of kills two birds with one stone.

428
00:23:02,790 --> 00:23:07,030
Very important to know that splenectomy kills two birds with one stone,

429
00:23:07,030 --> 00:23:09,710
but it's only performed in refractory cases.

430
00:23:10,210 --> 00:23:13,010
So that's the first disorder that we need to be familiar with.

431
00:23:13,310 --> 00:23:15,290
The second disorder that we want to talk about

432
00:23:15,290 --> 00:23:18,630
is called microangiopathic hemolytic anemia.

433
00:23:19,770 --> 00:23:22,630
It's kind of a mouthful, but if you understand the words,

434
00:23:22,850 --> 00:23:24,090
it's all downhill from there.

435
00:23:26,130 --> 00:23:27,730
Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia.

436
00:23:28,290 --> 00:23:29,430
Now, what does this mean?

437
00:23:30,050 --> 00:23:31,690
Microangio means small blood vessels.

438
00:23:31,910 --> 00:23:33,870
So we're talking about the small blood vessels,

439
00:23:34,510 --> 00:23:37,650
and, of course, these small blood vessels are lined by endothelium.

440
00:23:38,370 --> 00:23:42,250
And then pathic means we've got a pathology in the small blood vessels.

441
00:23:42,990 --> 00:23:45,030
Microangiopathic, pathology in the small blood vessels.

442
00:23:45,530 --> 00:23:48,070
And that pathology is resulting in a hemolytic anemia.

443
00:23:48,510 --> 00:23:52,030
Now, the idea here is that in this particular set of disorders,

444
00:23:52,550 --> 00:23:55,650
you have something called a platelet microthrombus.

445
00:23:55,650 --> 00:23:58,830
So for some reason, and I'll talk about the reasons in a minute,

446
00:23:59,190 --> 00:24:02,450
you get an abnormal aggregation of platelets

447
00:24:02,450 --> 00:24:04,330
forming a platelet microthrombus.

448
00:24:04,570 --> 00:24:06,370
Now, that platelet microthrombus,

449
00:24:06,810 --> 00:24:08,290
guess who has to pass across it?

450
00:24:08,930 --> 00:24:09,830
The red blood cell.

451
00:24:10,190 --> 00:24:13,790
So as the red blood cell crosses that platelet microthrombus,

452
00:24:13,850 --> 00:24:16,070
which is partially occluding the blood vessel,

453
00:24:16,470 --> 00:24:18,530
you're going to get shearing of the blood vessel.

454
00:24:18,870 --> 00:24:19,210
Oh, sorry.

455
00:24:19,290 --> 00:24:21,170
You're going to get shearing of the red blood cell,

456
00:24:21,490 --> 00:24:23,190
which is called a schistocyte.

457
00:24:23,190 --> 00:24:26,110
But that's going to obviously result in hemolysis

458
00:24:26,110 --> 00:24:28,330
because you've now lyced the red blood cell.

459
00:24:28,450 --> 00:24:29,190
You've destroyed it.

460
00:24:29,470 --> 00:24:31,950
So that's called hemolysis when you destroy the red blood cell.

461
00:24:32,230 --> 00:24:34,170
Obviously, here it's occurring within the blood vessel,

462
00:24:34,350 --> 00:24:37,070
and that's why the patients get a hemolytic anemia.

463
00:24:37,630 --> 00:24:42,310
So a hemolytic anemia due to pathology within the small blood vessel.

464
00:24:42,850 --> 00:24:45,390
Again, microangiopathic hemolytic anemia.

465
00:24:46,070 --> 00:24:48,330
Now, what are the complications going to be?

466
00:24:48,330 --> 00:24:52,250
So why don't we start by asking why am I talking about this disorder

467
00:24:52,250 --> 00:24:55,550
in the context of primary hemostatic problems?

468
00:24:55,990 --> 00:25:00,310
Well, as these microthrombi are formed, we're going to consume platelets.

469
00:25:00,770 --> 00:25:04,470
So you're going to use up platelets when these abnormal microthrombi are formed.

470
00:25:04,830 --> 00:25:05,990
And so that's what I say in your book,

471
00:25:06,070 --> 00:25:08,970
that platelets are consumed in the formation of microthrombi.

472
00:25:09,230 --> 00:25:11,430
And, of course, then the red blood cells will be sheared

473
00:25:11,430 --> 00:25:15,990
as they cross those microthrombi, resulting in the formation of schistocytes.

474
00:25:15,990 --> 00:25:19,930
Here's a very high-yield picture, shows up on examinations all the time.

475
00:25:20,230 --> 00:25:22,270
There are a lot of contexts in which we can see this,

476
00:25:22,410 --> 00:25:25,350
but at this point we're going to focus on two disorders.

477
00:25:25,930 --> 00:25:27,290
You'll hear about them in a second.

478
00:25:27,590 --> 00:25:29,670
But this is the schistocyte.

479
00:25:29,950 --> 00:25:34,210
This is the cell that has been sheared as it went across the microthrombus.

480
00:25:34,750 --> 00:25:38,510
And classically, you can see that it's kind of got two points at either end,

481
00:25:38,910 --> 00:25:41,250
and that's a classic morphologic finding.

482
00:25:41,570 --> 00:25:42,490
It looks like a helmet.

483
00:25:42,790 --> 00:25:44,310
Sometimes they call it a helmet cell.

484
00:25:44,310 --> 00:25:47,970
Anyway, this is an indication that the patient's got some sort of

485
00:25:47,970 --> 00:25:50,710
microangiopathic hemolytic anemia going on.

486
00:25:51,210 --> 00:25:55,230
Now, remember that I didn't give you the reason why these microthrombi are occurring.

487
00:25:55,670 --> 00:25:58,470
And there's two classic disorders in which we're going to see

488
00:25:58,470 --> 00:26:00,650
a microangiopathic hemolytic anemia.

489
00:26:01,090 --> 00:26:06,130
The first is TTP, also called thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura.

490
00:26:06,450 --> 00:26:10,550
And the second is HUS, also known as hemolytic uremic syndrome.

491
00:26:10,550 --> 00:26:16,250
In thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, the problem that arises, again,

492
00:26:16,370 --> 00:26:18,890
is it's the same story that I've already told.

493
00:26:19,210 --> 00:26:20,430
You've got this blood vessel.

494
00:26:20,890 --> 00:26:23,550
And within this blood vessel, you begin to form platelet microthrombi.

495
00:26:24,510 --> 00:26:28,050
Now, what's important to note is why those platelet microthrombi are forming

496
00:26:28,050 --> 00:26:32,110
is that in TTP, or thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura,

497
00:26:32,230 --> 00:26:33,110
look at the name again.

498
00:26:33,230 --> 00:26:35,490
Thrombotic, you're forming these platelet microthrombi.

499
00:26:35,490 --> 00:26:38,630
Thrombocytopenic, that's using up the platelets,

500
00:26:38,710 --> 00:26:40,510
and that's why you get thrombocytopenia.

501
00:26:40,930 --> 00:26:43,310
Purpura, the patients then get bleeds in the skin,

502
00:26:43,390 --> 00:26:45,770
which are purpura, because they've got low platelets.

503
00:26:45,830 --> 00:26:48,030
And one of the signs of low platelets is skin bleeding,

504
00:26:48,150 --> 00:26:50,530
and one of the types of skin bleeding would be purpura.

505
00:26:51,130 --> 00:26:52,710
All right, so that's where the name comes from.

506
00:26:53,110 --> 00:26:57,010
Now, the idea here, and the reason why the patients get these microthrombi,

507
00:26:57,010 --> 00:27:02,370
is this is due to a decrease in an enzyme called ADAMS-TS13.

508
00:27:02,370 --> 00:27:06,370
So that is the enzyme that's decreased in TTP.

509
00:27:06,910 --> 00:27:08,350
Now, what is ADAMS-TS13?

510
00:27:08,950 --> 00:27:13,830
Well, remember that normally when you produce your von Willebrand's factor,

511
00:27:13,930 --> 00:27:15,350
you produce it as multimers.

512
00:27:15,890 --> 00:27:21,190
And those multimers need to be degraded over time,

513
00:27:21,230 --> 00:27:22,710
and the way by which we degrade them

514
00:27:22,710 --> 00:27:24,970
is to chop them up into smaller monomers.

515
00:27:25,370 --> 00:27:28,270
And the enzyme that's necessary to chop up those multimers

516
00:27:28,270 --> 00:27:31,450
into smaller monomers is called ADAMS-TS13.

517
00:27:31,450 --> 00:27:33,670
Let me just illustrate this for you.

518
00:27:33,990 --> 00:27:36,810
If you've got von Willebrand's factor here,

519
00:27:37,190 --> 00:27:38,110
and it's a multimer,

520
00:27:38,250 --> 00:27:41,030
it's got another molecule of von Willebrand's factor here,

521
00:27:41,130 --> 00:27:43,650
and another molecule of von Willebrand's factor here,

522
00:27:43,870 --> 00:27:46,610
the way by which you're going to eventually degrade this molecule

523
00:27:46,610 --> 00:27:49,050
is to chop these things away one by one,

524
00:27:49,110 --> 00:27:50,570
which allows for eventual degradation.

525
00:27:51,190 --> 00:27:54,150
If you don't have the enzyme necessary to chop these things away,

526
00:27:54,330 --> 00:27:55,410
you can't degrade it,

527
00:27:55,410 --> 00:27:58,010
and so it begins to get a pileup,

528
00:27:58,010 --> 00:28:02,750
and these large multimers can lead to abnormal platelet adhesion.

529
00:28:02,950 --> 00:28:06,650
Remember that von Willebrand's factor is necessary for platelet adhesion,

530
00:28:06,810 --> 00:28:10,450
for the platelets to actually bind to the subendothelial collagen.

531
00:28:10,930 --> 00:28:13,030
So you'll get this abnormal platelet adhesion,

532
00:28:13,470 --> 00:28:17,090
and that will then result in the platelets sort of forming these microthrombi.

533
00:28:17,090 --> 00:28:19,690
So very high yield take-home point,

534
00:28:20,210 --> 00:28:25,790
TTP is due to Adams TS-13 deficiency,

535
00:28:26,150 --> 00:28:28,050
or a decrease in Adams TS-13.

536
00:28:28,350 --> 00:28:31,150
And that's what I've highlighted here in your text,

537
00:28:31,210 --> 00:28:34,450
where I say that TTP is due to decreased Adams TS-13.

538
00:28:34,950 --> 00:28:37,130
I tell you in the text that Adams TS-13

539
00:28:37,130 --> 00:28:40,410
normally cleaves von Willebrand's multimers into monomers

540
00:28:40,410 --> 00:28:41,670
so that it can be degraded.

541
00:28:41,910 --> 00:28:44,210
If you don't have the ability to degrade these,

542
00:28:44,250 --> 00:28:46,310
you get large, uncleaved multimers,

543
00:28:46,310 --> 00:28:49,270
which then leads to abnormal platelet adhesion,

544
00:28:49,730 --> 00:28:51,170
resulting in microthrombi.

545
00:28:51,530 --> 00:28:54,210
Again, you recall that adhesion is the step

546
00:28:54,210 --> 00:28:57,650
in which platelets use GP-1b to bind von Willebrand's factor

547
00:28:57,650 --> 00:28:59,690
to stick to the blood vessel.

548
00:29:00,270 --> 00:29:01,730
Now, we can then ask the question,

549
00:29:01,890 --> 00:29:04,850
why, what's causing this decrease in Adams TS-13?

550
00:29:05,430 --> 00:29:07,810
And the answer is that you can get a genetic defect

551
00:29:07,810 --> 00:29:10,050
in which you have a decrease in Adams TS-13,

552
00:29:10,310 --> 00:29:14,230
but the more common reason that you would have a decrease in Adams TS-13

553
00:29:14,230 --> 00:29:18,350
is because you produce an autoantibody against Adams TS-13,

554
00:29:18,570 --> 00:29:21,310
which then results in destruction of Adams TS-13.

555
00:29:21,870 --> 00:29:24,010
And so the classic patient that develops TTP

556
00:29:24,010 --> 00:29:27,750
is an adult female who, as an autoimmune process,

557
00:29:27,910 --> 00:29:29,310
develops an autoantibody,

558
00:29:29,610 --> 00:29:33,190
and that autoantibody then destroys Adams TS-13.

559
00:29:33,190 --> 00:29:35,930
So the classic patient is an adult female.

560
00:29:36,990 --> 00:29:39,150
Now, a second way by which we can get

561
00:29:39,150 --> 00:29:41,890
a microangiopathic hemolytic anemia

562
00:29:41,890 --> 00:29:44,190
is a disorder called HUS.

563
00:29:44,870 --> 00:29:49,550
HUS refers to hemolytic uremic syndrome.

564
00:29:50,110 --> 00:29:53,270
Now, the hemolytic here implies that you've got platelet microthrombi

565
00:29:53,270 --> 00:29:54,190
that results in hemolysis

566
00:29:54,830 --> 00:29:56,610
with shearing of the red blood cells

567
00:29:56,610 --> 00:29:58,070
and the production of schistocytes.

568
00:29:58,590 --> 00:30:02,190
The uremic syndrome implies that this is going to occur

569
00:30:02,190 --> 00:30:03,830
predominantly within the kidney,

570
00:30:04,090 --> 00:30:07,110
causing kidney damage, which will then result in uremia.

571
00:30:07,470 --> 00:30:09,010
And I'm going to talk about the clinical findings

572
00:30:09,010 --> 00:30:10,170
of both of these disorders,

573
00:30:10,390 --> 00:30:13,690
both TTP and HUS, in a moment.

574
00:30:14,230 --> 00:30:15,670
Now, in this particular case,

575
00:30:15,730 --> 00:30:17,430
what's happening is you've got a blood vessel.

576
00:30:17,430 --> 00:30:19,430
Of course, the blood vessel is lined by endothelium,

577
00:30:20,090 --> 00:30:21,850
which sits on top of a basement membrane.

578
00:30:22,310 --> 00:30:25,350
And here you're forming these platelet microthrombi.

579
00:30:25,410 --> 00:30:27,430
Again, I emphasize platelet microthrombi,

580
00:30:28,010 --> 00:30:29,310
just platelets are present here.

581
00:30:29,810 --> 00:30:31,910
And when you get these platelet microthrombi,

582
00:30:32,050 --> 00:30:33,410
you can ask the question, why?

583
00:30:33,750 --> 00:30:36,170
Well, in HUS, the reason why the patients

584
00:30:36,170 --> 00:30:37,650
get the platelet microthrombi

585
00:30:37,650 --> 00:30:40,050
is that they get an infection

586
00:30:40,050 --> 00:30:41,590
with a very special bug

587
00:30:41,590 --> 00:30:43,090
that you have to be familiar with.

588
00:30:43,130 --> 00:30:44,290
It's a very high-yield bug,

589
00:30:44,290 --> 00:30:47,870
and it's called E. coli 015787.

590
00:30:48,690 --> 00:30:51,630
So patients get this E. coli 015787,

591
00:30:51,630 --> 00:30:55,810
and that then results in a varotoxin

592
00:30:55,810 --> 00:30:57,190
being produced by the bug.

593
00:30:57,290 --> 00:30:58,890
It's called E. coli varotoxin,

594
00:30:58,990 --> 00:31:00,970
which damages the endothelial cells.

595
00:31:01,050 --> 00:31:02,790
And when it damages the endothelial cells,

596
00:31:03,050 --> 00:31:04,590
you get platelet microthrombi,

597
00:31:04,710 --> 00:31:07,150
particularly in the kidneys, but also in the brain.

598
00:31:07,650 --> 00:31:09,050
They get platelet microthrombi,

599
00:31:09,050 --> 00:31:10,610
and those platelet microthrombi

600
00:31:10,610 --> 00:31:13,510
will then result in a hemolysis

601
00:31:13,510 --> 00:31:15,390
because the red blood cells will get sheared,

602
00:31:15,490 --> 00:31:16,550
producing schistocytes,

603
00:31:16,970 --> 00:31:18,170
a thrombocytopenia

604
00:31:18,170 --> 00:31:20,170
because the platelets are getting used up.

605
00:31:20,650 --> 00:31:20,790
All right?

606
00:31:21,090 --> 00:31:23,770
And so this is a classic mechanism

607
00:31:23,770 --> 00:31:25,270
by which patients get HUS.

608
00:31:25,270 --> 00:31:31,170
Again, HUS, the key mechanism is E. coli 015787,

609
00:31:31,550 --> 00:31:32,470
super high yield.

610
00:31:32,870 --> 00:31:34,050
Now the next discussion is

611
00:31:34,050 --> 00:31:36,270
who gets E. coli 015787.

612
00:31:36,710 --> 00:31:38,630
Well, that's classically seen in children.

613
00:31:39,050 --> 00:31:42,230
Who get exposure to undercooked beef.

614
00:31:42,710 --> 00:31:47,130
That's the way by which this bug can arise.

615
00:31:47,530 --> 00:31:49,130
That results in a dysentery

616
00:31:49,130 --> 00:31:51,690
in which they get mucousy diarrhea.

617
00:31:52,530 --> 00:31:55,990
And again, that's due to E. coli 015787.

618
00:31:56,610 --> 00:31:59,310
Again, I told you that the bug produces a varotoxin

619
00:31:59,310 --> 00:32:01,610
which damages the endothelial cells

620
00:32:01,610 --> 00:32:03,270
resulting in platelet microthrombi.

621
00:32:03,630 --> 00:32:05,930
Interestingly, the most recent understanding

622
00:32:05,930 --> 00:32:07,390
of this particular disorder

623
00:32:07,390 --> 00:32:08,870
is that that varotoxin

624
00:32:08,870 --> 00:32:10,370
by damaging the endothelial cells

625
00:32:10,370 --> 00:32:13,370
also decreases the amount of ADAMS-TS13.

626
00:32:13,750 --> 00:32:16,790
So the mechanism here is kind of similar to TTP,

627
00:32:16,990 --> 00:32:17,910
which we just talked about.

628
00:32:18,150 --> 00:32:19,010
I guess in TTP,

629
00:32:19,270 --> 00:32:20,810
you get autoimmune destruction

630
00:32:20,810 --> 00:32:22,430
of the ADAMS-TS13.

631
00:32:22,970 --> 00:32:24,310
And in this particular disorder,

632
00:32:24,610 --> 00:32:26,610
it's the E. coli 015787

633
00:32:26,610 --> 00:32:28,470
that damages the endothelial cells,

634
00:32:28,550 --> 00:32:30,990
which also actually leads to a problem

635
00:32:30,990 --> 00:32:32,970
or a decrease in ADAMS-TS13.

636
00:32:33,310 --> 00:32:34,970
But that's sort of the latest information

637
00:32:34,970 --> 00:32:37,470
concerning this disorder.

638
00:32:37,970 --> 00:32:40,010
Now, the clinical findings are very similar

639
00:32:40,010 --> 00:32:41,390
in TTP and HUS.

640
00:32:41,950 --> 00:32:44,370
So if it's thrombotic, thrombocytopenic, purpura,

641
00:32:44,630 --> 00:32:46,130
or hemolytic uremic syndrome,

642
00:32:46,570 --> 00:32:49,690
these are both microangiopathic hemolytic anemias,

643
00:32:49,810 --> 00:32:50,750
as I've already explained,

644
00:32:51,110 --> 00:32:52,630
and the clinical findings are very similar.

645
00:32:52,630 --> 00:32:54,470
So they get skin and mucosal bleeding.

646
00:32:54,670 --> 00:32:56,290
Why would they get skin and mucosal bleeding?

647
00:32:56,410 --> 00:32:57,590
Well, in both cases,

648
00:32:57,590 --> 00:32:59,190
you're getting platelet microthrombi

649
00:32:59,190 --> 00:33:00,510
within the blood vessels, correct?

650
00:33:00,710 --> 00:33:03,050
The problem was that you've got these blood vessels

651
00:33:03,050 --> 00:33:05,270
and you get these platelet microthrombi in them.

652
00:33:05,610 --> 00:33:07,150
The platelet microthrombi result

653
00:33:07,150 --> 00:33:08,510
in the consumption of platelets.

654
00:33:08,610 --> 00:33:10,010
To form this microthrombus,

655
00:33:10,050 --> 00:33:10,990
you're going to use up platelets.

656
00:33:11,370 --> 00:33:12,530
And as you use up platelets,

657
00:33:12,610 --> 00:33:14,090
you're going to have skin and mucosal bleeding

658
00:33:14,090 --> 00:33:16,210
because you're getting thrombocytopenic.

659
00:33:16,610 --> 00:33:17,470
At the same time,

660
00:33:17,530 --> 00:33:20,790
the blood cells have to cross those platelet microthrombi.

661
00:33:20,850 --> 00:33:22,210
They're going to get sheared,

662
00:33:22,570 --> 00:33:24,750
and that's going to give you a microangiopathic,

663
00:33:24,870 --> 00:33:26,390
i.e. in the small blood vessels,

664
00:33:26,390 --> 00:33:27,670
hemolytic anemia.

665
00:33:27,890 --> 00:33:30,010
Hemolysis means the breaking up

666
00:33:30,010 --> 00:33:31,690
or the destruction of red blood cells.

667
00:33:32,150 --> 00:33:33,750
The patients also tend to have fever,

668
00:33:34,370 --> 00:33:37,250
and they get this whole process,

669
00:33:37,310 --> 00:33:38,510
this whole process of forming

670
00:33:38,510 --> 00:33:39,790
these platelet microthrombi

671
00:33:39,790 --> 00:33:41,470
and shearing the red blood cells

672
00:33:41,470 --> 00:33:42,270
within the tissues

673
00:33:42,270 --> 00:33:44,590
occurs primarily within two tissues.

674
00:33:45,010 --> 00:33:46,510
Number one, it occurs in the kidney.

675
00:33:46,950 --> 00:33:48,610
So in both of these diseases,

676
00:33:48,790 --> 00:33:50,290
TTP and HUS,

677
00:33:50,490 --> 00:33:51,890
you're going to have renal insufficiency,

678
00:33:51,890 --> 00:33:55,330
and it also occurs within the CNS.

679
00:33:55,650 --> 00:33:57,130
And so in both of these disorders,

680
00:33:57,610 --> 00:33:58,770
TTP and HUS,

681
00:33:59,190 --> 00:34:00,770
you can have CNS abnormalities.

682
00:34:01,170 --> 00:34:03,250
What's important to note is that in TTP,

683
00:34:03,950 --> 00:34:06,410
the predominant problem is CNS,

684
00:34:06,810 --> 00:34:07,750
and in HUS,

685
00:34:08,110 --> 00:34:09,890
the predominant problem is renal.

686
00:34:10,310 --> 00:34:11,670
And the way you remember that

687
00:34:11,670 --> 00:34:12,630
is that in HUS,

688
00:34:13,010 --> 00:34:15,470
you know that the letters stand for

689
00:34:15,470 --> 00:34:17,230
hemolytic uremic syndrome,

690
00:34:17,230 --> 00:34:18,510
and remember that uremia

691
00:34:18,510 --> 00:34:21,210
is a sign of a renal problem.

692
00:34:21,930 --> 00:34:23,630
The laboratory findings are very straightforward

693
00:34:23,630 --> 00:34:25,070
if you understand the disorders.

694
00:34:25,570 --> 00:34:26,950
Basically, these patients are going to have

695
00:34:26,950 --> 00:34:28,110
thrombocytopenia,

696
00:34:28,250 --> 00:34:29,230
a decrease in platelets.

697
00:34:29,390 --> 00:34:30,630
Again, they're using them

698
00:34:30,630 --> 00:34:32,610
in the formation of the microthrombi.

699
00:34:33,050 --> 00:34:35,070
There's going to be an increased bleeding time

700
00:34:35,070 --> 00:34:37,430
because they don't have good numbers of platelets,

701
00:34:37,530 --> 00:34:39,250
and so rather than being the normal

702
00:34:39,250 --> 00:34:40,290
two to seven minutes,

703
00:34:40,550 --> 00:34:41,490
the bleeding time might be

704
00:34:41,490 --> 00:34:43,390
10 or 15 minutes, for example.

705
00:34:43,850 --> 00:34:45,210
Now, very important to note,

706
00:34:45,310 --> 00:34:46,430
and super high yield,

707
00:34:46,430 --> 00:34:49,650
is that the PT-PTT are classically normal,

708
00:34:50,110 --> 00:34:51,730
especially for exam purposes.

709
00:34:52,330 --> 00:34:54,610
Classically, not necessarily always,

710
00:34:54,770 --> 00:34:55,550
but classically,

711
00:34:55,910 --> 00:34:57,430
the PT-PTT will be normal.

712
00:34:57,810 --> 00:34:58,610
And the idea here is

713
00:34:58,610 --> 00:35:01,430
you haven't activated the coagulation cascade.

714
00:35:01,990 --> 00:35:04,110
When these microthrombi are forming,

715
00:35:04,950 --> 00:35:05,990
what's happening is

716
00:35:05,990 --> 00:35:07,610
you're forming these platelet microthrombi,

717
00:35:07,690 --> 00:35:09,550
but you're not going to the next step

718
00:35:09,550 --> 00:35:11,630
where you actually activate

719
00:35:11,630 --> 00:35:12,750
the coagulation cascade,

720
00:35:13,150 --> 00:35:14,170
and you get cross-linking.

721
00:35:14,170 --> 00:35:16,470
So, the only thing that's happening

722
00:35:16,470 --> 00:35:19,670
is that the platelets are actually aggregating

723
00:35:19,670 --> 00:35:20,370
at that site.

724
00:35:20,470 --> 00:35:21,810
Well, maybe I should say more correctly,

725
00:35:22,050 --> 00:35:24,510
there's adhesion of platelets at that site.

726
00:35:24,970 --> 00:35:28,290
And so, the PT-PTT will be normal

727
00:35:28,290 --> 00:35:29,630
in this disorder.

728
00:35:29,970 --> 00:35:31,230
And that's, again, very important.

729
00:35:31,650 --> 00:35:33,150
The patients will also have anemia

730
00:35:33,150 --> 00:35:34,490
with schistocytes,

731
00:35:34,750 --> 00:35:36,750
and there'll be increased megakaryocytes

732
00:35:36,750 --> 00:35:37,950
on bone marrow biopsy.

733
00:35:38,190 --> 00:35:38,490
Why?

734
00:35:38,490 --> 00:35:41,470
Because as the platelets are being consumed,

735
00:35:42,070 --> 00:35:43,350
the bone marrow will recognize

736
00:35:43,350 --> 00:35:45,050
that there's less platelets in the blood,

737
00:35:45,410 --> 00:35:46,910
and the megakaryocytes will begin

738
00:35:46,910 --> 00:35:48,350
to produce extra platelets

739
00:35:48,350 --> 00:35:50,910
to try to help out the body.

740
00:35:51,370 --> 00:35:52,770
And those extra platelets

741
00:35:52,770 --> 00:35:54,490
have to be produced by extra megakaryocytes.

742
00:35:55,150 --> 00:35:56,810
And so, you'll get hyperplasia

743
00:35:56,810 --> 00:35:57,490
of the megakaryocytes,

744
00:35:58,490 --> 00:36:00,570
and you'll see that on bone marrow biopsy.

745
00:36:01,290 --> 00:36:02,830
The treatment of these disorders,

746
00:36:03,070 --> 00:36:04,230
in particular TTP,

747
00:36:04,550 --> 00:36:06,490
involves plasmapheresis and corticosteroids.

748
00:36:06,490 --> 00:36:09,410
Now, the idea here is that in TTP,

749
00:36:09,710 --> 00:36:11,650
the disorder is due to an autoantibody.

750
00:36:12,410 --> 00:36:13,470
Plasmapheresis is a mechanism

751
00:36:13,470 --> 00:36:15,930
by which you can remove proteins from the blood,

752
00:36:16,310 --> 00:36:18,130
and so you can remove, for example,

753
00:36:18,810 --> 00:36:20,250
by doing plasmapheresis,

754
00:36:20,370 --> 00:36:21,970
you could remove the autoantibody

755
00:36:21,970 --> 00:36:23,650
against ADAMS-TS13,

756
00:36:23,870 --> 00:36:25,050
which was leading to the problem.

757
00:36:25,450 --> 00:36:26,170
At the same time,

758
00:36:26,230 --> 00:36:27,450
by giving corticosteroids,

759
00:36:27,510 --> 00:36:29,050
what you're doing is decreasing production

760
00:36:29,050 --> 00:36:30,030
of the autoantibody,

761
00:36:30,270 --> 00:36:32,390
so that would also be helpful in TTP.

762
00:36:33,410 --> 00:36:34,530
Now, the next set of disorders

763
00:36:34,530 --> 00:36:35,650
that we want to talk about

764
00:36:35,650 --> 00:36:38,730
are those of quality of the platelets.

765
00:36:38,810 --> 00:36:39,950
Remember, I told you that there are

766
00:36:39,950 --> 00:36:41,050
quantitative disorders

767
00:36:41,050 --> 00:36:42,830
in which the number of platelets is down.

768
00:36:43,130 --> 00:36:44,770
For example, in ITP,

769
00:36:45,090 --> 00:36:46,970
the whole idea was that we were losing

770
00:36:46,970 --> 00:36:50,050
our platelet number due to an IgG antibody.

771
00:36:50,510 --> 00:36:53,190
For example, in TTP and in HUS,

772
00:36:53,490 --> 00:36:55,890
these microangiopathic hemolytic anemias,

773
00:36:56,310 --> 00:36:57,890
we were losing our platelet count

774
00:36:57,890 --> 00:36:59,650
due to use of the platelets

775
00:36:59,650 --> 00:37:01,150
to form the microthrombi.

776
00:37:01,150 --> 00:37:04,410
So those were examples of quantitative problems.

777
00:37:04,530 --> 00:37:07,630
Now we're talking about qualitative problems,

778
00:37:07,750 --> 00:37:09,970
problems in which there is an issue

779
00:37:09,970 --> 00:37:11,890
with the quality of the platelets.

780
00:37:12,110 --> 00:37:14,210
The first is called Bernard-Soulier syndrome,

781
00:37:14,430 --> 00:37:17,050
and this is a genetic GP1B deficiency.

782
00:37:17,590 --> 00:37:20,110
Now, of course, if GP1B is deficient,

783
00:37:20,270 --> 00:37:21,650
platelet adhesion cannot occur.

784
00:37:21,750 --> 00:37:22,550
Let's remind ourselves

785
00:37:22,550 --> 00:37:24,230
that when we have this blood vessel

786
00:37:24,230 --> 00:37:26,070
and there is damage to the blood vessel,

787
00:37:26,070 --> 00:37:27,310
the first thing that's going to happen

788
00:37:27,310 --> 00:37:28,890
is transient vasoconstriction.

789
00:37:28,890 --> 00:37:30,770
After transient vasoconstriction,

790
00:37:30,830 --> 00:37:32,270
we're going to have von Willebrand's factor

791
00:37:32,270 --> 00:37:34,070
bind the subendothelial collagen.

792
00:37:34,530 --> 00:37:36,030
Remember, where does von Willebrand's factor

793
00:37:36,030 --> 00:37:36,570
come from?

794
00:37:37,390 --> 00:37:39,430
Number one, the alpha granule of the platelet,

795
00:37:39,570 --> 00:37:41,350
and number two, and more important,

796
00:37:41,650 --> 00:37:44,390
the libel-pilotti body of the endothelial cell.

797
00:37:44,790 --> 00:37:46,570
Now, of course, that von Willebrand's factor

798
00:37:46,570 --> 00:37:47,990
isn't going to bind that exposed

799
00:37:47,990 --> 00:37:49,190
subendothelial collagen,

800
00:37:49,330 --> 00:37:50,870
so it can act as a linker molecule

801
00:37:50,870 --> 00:37:52,890
for the platelets to adhese.

802
00:37:53,550 --> 00:37:54,250
Remember that?

803
00:37:54,330 --> 00:37:56,890
To adhese via a receptor called GP1B.

804
00:37:56,890 --> 00:38:00,550
Now, if the patients don't have that receptor

805
00:38:00,550 --> 00:38:02,130
because there's a genetic deficiency,

806
00:38:02,390 --> 00:38:04,150
i.e. Bernard-Soulier syndrome,

807
00:38:04,630 --> 00:38:06,010
obviously the patients are going to have

808
00:38:06,010 --> 00:38:08,630
bleeding problems because they can't bind

809
00:38:08,630 --> 00:38:09,950
the von Willebrand's factor

810
00:38:09,950 --> 00:38:12,190
and they can't undergo this important step

811
00:38:12,190 --> 00:38:13,030
of adhesion.

812
00:38:13,710 --> 00:38:15,130
So let's see what we say in the book.

813
00:38:15,470 --> 00:38:17,030
Genetic GP1B deficiency,

814
00:38:17,350 --> 00:38:18,650
platelet adhesion is impaired.

815
00:38:19,010 --> 00:38:20,230
What will the blood smear show?

816
00:38:20,490 --> 00:38:22,550
It will show thrombocytopenia

817
00:38:22,550 --> 00:38:24,430
with enlarged platelets.

818
00:38:24,430 --> 00:38:26,010
Now, the reason why these patients

819
00:38:26,010 --> 00:38:27,350
get mild thrombocytopenia

820
00:38:27,350 --> 00:38:30,830
is because if GP1B is not functioning well,

821
00:38:31,110 --> 00:38:33,010
the platelets tend to not live as long

822
00:38:33,010 --> 00:38:34,790
and so the platelets get destroyed.

823
00:38:35,190 --> 00:38:37,990
So that's why they get mild thrombocytopenia

824
00:38:37,990 --> 00:38:39,770
and they get enlarged platelets

825
00:38:39,770 --> 00:38:41,890
because they're producing platelets

826
00:38:41,890 --> 00:38:44,130
a little bit more immature

827
00:38:44,130 --> 00:38:45,770
than they would normally produce

828
00:38:45,770 --> 00:38:46,570
from the bone marrow.

829
00:38:46,950 --> 00:38:48,290
And so you can think about that

830
00:38:48,290 --> 00:38:49,970
being the reason why the platelets

831
00:38:49,970 --> 00:38:50,810
might be enlarged.

832
00:38:50,810 --> 00:38:54,070
But perhaps a funny way to remember this

833
00:38:54,070 --> 00:38:55,150
and one way in which a student

834
00:38:55,150 --> 00:38:56,310
sort of reminded me once

835
00:38:56,310 --> 00:38:59,350
is that in Bernard-Soulier disease,

836
00:38:59,570 --> 00:39:02,270
you get big suckers, big suckers,

837
00:39:02,370 --> 00:39:04,590
meaning he was referring in that particular case

838
00:39:04,590 --> 00:39:05,910
to the enlarged platelets.

839
00:39:06,050 --> 00:39:07,570
Whatever helps you to remember this,

840
00:39:07,770 --> 00:39:08,670
that's the key thing,

841
00:39:08,710 --> 00:39:09,550
that you need to remember

842
00:39:09,550 --> 00:39:12,190
that there is enlarged platelets

843
00:39:12,190 --> 00:39:13,110
on blood smear.

844
00:39:13,110 --> 00:39:15,030
Now the next of the disorders

845
00:39:15,030 --> 00:39:17,790
is called Glansman's thrombasthenia

846
00:39:17,790 --> 00:39:21,610
and this is a genetic defect in GP2B3A.

847
00:39:22,090 --> 00:39:23,210
And guess what's going to happen here?

848
00:39:23,390 --> 00:39:25,750
Platelet aggregation is going to be impaired.

849
00:39:26,090 --> 00:39:27,250
So what's platelet aggregation?

850
00:39:27,370 --> 00:39:28,710
Well remember that if you've got

851
00:39:28,710 --> 00:39:29,890
a damaged blood vessel,

852
00:39:30,310 --> 00:39:31,970
then obviously von Willebrand's factor

853
00:39:31,970 --> 00:39:32,990
is going to come in here

854
00:39:32,990 --> 00:39:35,510
and bind to the subendothelial collagen.

855
00:39:35,970 --> 00:39:37,770
The platelets will then bind

856
00:39:37,770 --> 00:39:39,490
this von Willebrand's factor

857
00:39:39,490 --> 00:39:41,750
using GP1B.

858
00:39:41,750 --> 00:39:43,290
Now of course,

859
00:39:43,450 --> 00:39:44,630
binding of von Willebrand's factor

860
00:39:44,630 --> 00:39:46,810
by GP1B activates the platelets.

861
00:39:47,230 --> 00:39:49,510
They then undergo degranulation

862
00:39:49,510 --> 00:39:50,390
and shape change

863
00:39:50,390 --> 00:39:51,310
and you know that the platelets

864
00:39:51,310 --> 00:39:52,690
are going to release ADP

865
00:39:52,690 --> 00:39:54,930
and they're also going to release

866
00:39:54,930 --> 00:39:56,670
something called TXA2

867
00:39:56,670 --> 00:39:57,550
which is a derivative

868
00:39:57,550 --> 00:39:59,110
of platelet cyclooxygenase.

869
00:39:59,510 --> 00:40:01,390
Now remember that the role of ADP

870
00:40:01,390 --> 00:40:01,910
was what?

871
00:40:02,170 --> 00:40:03,670
Was to cause the platelet

872
00:40:03,670 --> 00:40:06,130
to flip out this particular receptor

873
00:40:06,130 --> 00:40:07,550
and to express a receptor

874
00:40:07,550 --> 00:40:11,670
called GP2B3A.

875
00:40:11,750 --> 00:40:14,910
And you know that that particular receptor

876
00:40:14,910 --> 00:40:18,970
GP2B3A

877
00:40:18,970 --> 00:40:20,890
that that particular receptor

878
00:40:20,890 --> 00:40:24,070
is necessary for the aggregation

879
00:40:24,070 --> 00:40:25,190
of other platelets.

880
00:40:25,290 --> 00:40:26,370
And remember that the next platelet

881
00:40:26,370 --> 00:40:27,010
would come in.

882
00:40:27,090 --> 00:40:29,410
It would also express GP2B3A

883
00:40:29,410 --> 00:40:31,490
and the linking molecule here

884
00:40:31,490 --> 00:40:32,650
is fibrinogen.

885
00:40:33,390 --> 00:40:34,190
So if you...

886
00:40:34,190 --> 00:40:35,110
And that then allows

887
00:40:35,110 --> 00:40:36,530
for platelet aggregation.

888
00:40:36,530 --> 00:40:39,070
Now imagine if you don't have GP2B3A

889
00:40:39,070 --> 00:40:41,170
this particular part of the process

890
00:40:41,170 --> 00:40:41,830
can't occur.

891
00:40:41,970 --> 00:40:43,150
You can't have the platelets

892
00:40:43,150 --> 00:40:44,190
binding to each other

893
00:40:44,190 --> 00:40:45,410
and so you're not going to be able

894
00:40:45,410 --> 00:40:46,550
to aggregate the platelet

895
00:40:46,550 --> 00:40:47,790
and so that platelet plug

896
00:40:47,790 --> 00:40:49,570
is not going to form very well.

897
00:40:50,130 --> 00:40:51,090
Now another way by which

898
00:40:51,090 --> 00:40:53,030
you can have a qualitative,

899
00:40:53,470 --> 00:40:55,150
quality problem with the platelets

900
00:40:55,150 --> 00:40:57,330
is if a patient is taking aspirin.

901
00:40:57,630 --> 00:40:58,730
Remember that aspirin

902
00:40:58,730 --> 00:40:59,670
irreversibly inactivates

903
00:41:00,550 --> 00:41:00,670
cyclooxygenase.

904
00:41:00,670 --> 00:41:03,050
That's a high yield farm principle.

905
00:41:03,450 --> 00:41:04,670
That when you take aspirin

906
00:41:04,670 --> 00:41:06,390
that inactivates cyclooxygenase

907
00:41:06,390 --> 00:41:07,750
and it's irreversible,

908
00:41:07,910 --> 00:41:08,790
that inactivation.

909
00:41:09,350 --> 00:41:10,430
What's going to happen is

910
00:41:10,430 --> 00:41:11,410
remember again

911
00:41:11,410 --> 00:41:12,610
that when the platelets are

912
00:41:12,610 --> 00:41:13,450
you know,

913
00:41:13,590 --> 00:41:15,110
you've got von Willebrand's factor here

914
00:41:15,110 --> 00:41:16,390
and then the platelet binds

915
00:41:16,390 --> 00:41:17,570
via GP1B

916
00:41:17,570 --> 00:41:19,430
and then that is going to be called

917
00:41:19,430 --> 00:41:20,590
adhesion

918
00:41:20,590 --> 00:41:21,710
and then the next step

919
00:41:21,710 --> 00:41:22,830
is that you're going to get

920
00:41:22,830 --> 00:41:24,010
once this adhesion occurs

921
00:41:24,010 --> 00:41:24,890
is that you're going to get

922
00:41:24,890 --> 00:41:27,230
degranulation and release of mediators

923
00:41:27,230 --> 00:41:28,290
and one of the mediators

924
00:41:28,290 --> 00:41:29,130
was ADP

925
00:41:29,130 --> 00:41:30,310
which we've already talked about

926
00:41:30,310 --> 00:41:31,730
and another mediator

927
00:41:31,730 --> 00:41:32,830
was TXA2

928
00:41:32,830 --> 00:41:34,190
or thromboxyne2.

929
00:41:34,710 --> 00:41:35,510
I told you

930
00:41:35,510 --> 00:41:36,630
several times now

931
00:41:36,630 --> 00:41:37,890
that thromboxyne2

932
00:41:37,890 --> 00:41:38,830
is actually derived

933
00:41:38,830 --> 00:41:40,250
from platelet cyclooxygenase

934
00:41:40,250 --> 00:41:42,150
and this is an essential component

935
00:41:42,150 --> 00:41:43,090
of this reaction

936
00:41:43,090 --> 00:41:44,910
because it's thromboxyne2

937
00:41:44,910 --> 00:41:45,990
that sort of calls in

938
00:41:45,990 --> 00:41:46,650
the other platelets

939
00:41:46,650 --> 00:41:47,030
and says,

940
00:41:47,190 --> 00:41:47,530
hey guys,

941
00:41:47,630 --> 00:41:48,410
let's aggregate.

942
00:41:48,930 --> 00:41:49,090
Alright,

943
00:41:49,150 --> 00:41:49,990
let's join together.

944
00:41:50,510 --> 00:41:51,630
And so if you don't have

945
00:41:51,630 --> 00:41:52,670
platelet cyclooxygenase

946
00:41:52,670 --> 00:41:53,650
you're not going to be able

947
00:41:53,650 --> 00:41:55,450
to produce this aggregation step.

948
00:41:56,110 --> 00:41:57,550
And so that would result in

949
00:41:57,550 --> 00:41:59,470
a qualitative disorder

950
00:41:59,470 --> 00:42:00,250
of the platelets.

951
00:42:00,310 --> 00:42:01,450
So the quality of the platelets

952
00:42:01,450 --> 00:42:02,130
would be bad.

953
00:42:02,730 --> 00:42:04,130
And so platelet aggregation

954
00:42:04,130 --> 00:42:04,830
would not be able

955
00:42:04,830 --> 00:42:05,750
to occur properly.

956
00:42:06,290 --> 00:42:09,350
The last of the qualitative disorders

957
00:42:09,350 --> 00:42:10,390
that we're going to discuss

958
00:42:10,390 --> 00:42:11,250
is uremia.

959
00:42:11,590 --> 00:42:11,990
Uremia,

960
00:42:12,190 --> 00:42:12,910
you recall,

961
00:42:13,050 --> 00:42:15,250
is a state that a patient enters

962
00:42:15,250 --> 00:42:16,590
when they have

963
00:42:16,590 --> 00:42:18,150
poor kidney function.

964
00:42:18,350 --> 00:42:19,750
And that results in a buildup

965
00:42:19,750 --> 00:42:21,210
of nitrogenous waste products.

966
00:42:21,470 --> 00:42:22,670
So that's called uremia.

967
00:42:22,670 --> 00:42:23,730
And this uremia

968
00:42:23,730 --> 00:42:25,030
will disrupt platelet function

969
00:42:25,030 --> 00:42:27,050
by creating disorders

970
00:42:27,050 --> 00:42:28,090
of both adhesion

971
00:42:28,090 --> 00:42:29,670
and aggregation

972
00:42:29,670 --> 00:42:30,710
so that the platelets

973
00:42:30,710 --> 00:42:31,650
don't generally tend

974
00:42:31,650 --> 00:42:32,430
to function well

975
00:42:32,430 --> 00:42:33,850
when patients are uremic.

976
00:42:34,230 --> 00:42:35,270
And that is the end

977
00:42:35,270 --> 00:42:35,990
of our discussion

978
00:42:35,990 --> 00:42:36,670
of primary hemostasis.

979
00:42:37,450 --> 00:42:38,210
In the next section

980
00:42:38,210 --> 00:42:39,310
we're going to go into

981
00:42:39,310 --> 00:42:41,070
secondary hemostasis.

982
00:42:41,070 --> 00:42:41,730
Thank you.

983
00:42:43,270 --> 00:42:44,390
All right.

984
00:42:44,390 --> 00:42:45,030
All right.

